<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-ht-single-mean" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>A Single Mean</title>
  <p> 
    We've done three kinds of hypothesis test so far, but they have
    all involved proportions. Now I want to move on to statistics
    based on means. 
  </p>
  <p>
    So what is this kind of statistics? Let me recap the kinds of
    statistics we've seen so far in hypothesis testing and confidence
    intervals. A difference in proportion is exemplified by a
    controlled trial. An alternative hypothesis would be that vaccines
    can prevent polio. There are two proportions: the infected
    proportion of the control group and the infected proportion of the
    control group. The sample statistics is the difference in
    proportions. The null hypothesis is that there is no difference in
    the population, but the alternative hypothesis says there is a
    difference.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then we moved on to single proportions. Still a categorical
    variable, but no longer a treatment/control comparison. The
    alternative hypothesis is usually a threshold: at least
    <m>30\%</m> of the lodgepole pine trees in BC have been infected
    by the mountain pine beetle. The null hypothesis is no longer
    zero, but just the negation of the threshold. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Finally, we did <m>X^2</m> statistics for multiple proportions.
    This is again no longer a treatment/control comparison, but a
    comparison of many different categorical categories. We wanted to
    know if the categories in one variable substantially made a real
    difference in the other variable, all across the various multiple
    categories. The null hypotheses says that there is no variance:
    that the first category doesn't really effect the second. The
    alternative hypothesis is the opposite: the first category has an
    effect. Since there are multiple categories, that effect may be
    complicated, but the effect is present. 
  </p>
  <p>
    These were all statistics involving categorical variables. In the
    next three sections, we move on to numeric variables. We want to
    repeat all three of these patterns: single, diffrence and
    multiple, but now for means instead of proportions. The next three
    sections cover these three situations. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Though the approach to hypothesis testing is similar, working with
    numerical data is different in some important ways. The data is a
    bit more complicated than proportions. It's not just yes or no,
    success or failure. Instead,
    numeric variables take a whole range of values.  Therefore, the
    population itself is a whole distribution, not just a single
    percentage. We're trying to figure something out about a pretty
    complicated situation. The population distribution is, like the
    population parameter, the actual situation in the population. We
    have no idea what this looks like: it could be some strange kind
    of distribution, with peaks and valleys, patterns, whatever. There
    is no guarantee that it is a normal distribution, or any other
    kind as well. 
  </p>
  <p>
    So how do we investigate it? We'll, we use the tools we have for
    distributions. For now, I'll stick to two: mean and standard
    deviations. All distributions (not just normal distributions) have
    means and standard deviations. We'd like to use the sample to
    determine something about the population mean and the population
    standard deviation, with some confidence or by testing some
    hypothesis. This is what we are trying to do.
  </p>
  <p>
    Do be careful, now. This is a population distribution: it's how
    that actual unknown entire population looks for a certain
    variable. We'll be look at a sample and then looking at
    randomization as before. This will create sampling distributions.
    It's important to keep the difference in mind: there is a
    population distribution and a sampling distribution, and they are
    not at all the same.
  </p>
  <p>
    I will start with a investigating single mean. I have a numeric
    variable, and that is the only variable: there are no groups to
    compare. The population has a distribution and I want to
    investigate the mean of that distribution.  Since
    there is no natural comparison, like with a single proportion, I
    need a threshold. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Again, I'll use an example to set this up. We'll talk about the
    length of adult female blue whales. Say we managed to measure 20
    such whales in the wild, and we got the data in The following
    list.
  </p>
  <table xml:id="table-whale-data">
    <title>Length Data for Blue Whales</title>
    <tabular>
      <row>
        <cell> 24.3 </cell>
        <cell> 20.1 </cell>
        <cell> 25.4 </cell>
        <cell> 25.1 </cell>
        <cell> 19.3 </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell> 21.9 </cell>
        <cell> 22.9 </cell>
        <cell> 26.7 </cell>
        <cell> 19.3 </cell>
        <cell> 24.7 </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell> 25.3 </cell>
        <cell> 23.8 </cell>
        <cell> 23.9 </cell>
        <cell> 24.1 </cell>
        <cell> 24.9 </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell> 22.0 </cell>
        <cell> 22.1 </cell>
        <cell> 21.9 </cell>
        <cell> 24.9 </cell>
        <cell> 25.1  </cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>
  <p> 
    This is our data set. We want to figure out what we can say about
    the general female blue whale population based on this data.
    We're going to need a sample statistic. From these twenty values,
    I can calculate both the mean and the standard deviation. 
    The mean is 23.385, which I will write as <m>\bar{x}</m> for the
    rest of this section. The sample standard deviation is 2.116.
  </p>
  <p>
    That's my sample statistic. What are my hypotheses? I need a
    threshold. These particular researchers are interest in whether
    this mean, in the population, exceeds 21 metres. With this
    threshold, and the fact that the research question is about
    exceeding the threshold, the null hypothesis would be that the
    population mean is 21 metres or less. The alternative hypothesis
    states that the population mean does exceed 21 metres. 
  </p>
  <p>
    From the hypotheses, the process proceeds in the same way as
    before. I would need a randomization procees assuing the null
    hypothesis and, from that randomization process, I would calculate
    a sample distribution. Again, we don't want to do this
    randomization by hand: we want to rely on mathematical theory to
    provide the sampling distribution. And again, under reasonable
    condition, the central limit theorem will apply and the sampling
    distribution should be normal.
  </p>
  <p>
    I didn't talk that much about conditions for the central limit
    theorem with proportions. By the design of the randomization
    process, conditions were always satisfied other than sample size
    and we already know that very small samples lead to a hose of
    problems. However, there is a bit more to say with conditions for
    means. Now the sample is a distribution of numbers, so that
    analysis of distributions applies. In particular, I can identify
    outliers. One of the conditions for the central limit theorem
    applied to numerical variables is that the sample should avoid
    outliers. Moreover, the smaller the sample, the more outliers
    become a problem. For a large sample, only very extreme outliers
    will cause the central limit theorem to fail. For small samples,
    even modust outliers are a problem. 
  </p>
  <p>
    In my example, the sample is small: only <m>20</m> whales. (This
    is not surprising for this data, since finding and measuring
    whales is a difficult thing to do.) The data points <m>19.3</m>
    and <m>26.7</m> were the bottom and top of the range.  Looking at
    the list of numbers, these are both close to other values. I can
    pretty confidently say that these are not outliers, to my data
    should satisfy the condition of the central limit theorem. 
  </p>
  <p>
    So I should have a normal distribution for the sampling
    distribution. Well, not quite. This worry about outliers, even
    modest ones, inspires an adjustment to the mathematical theory.
    For numeric data, the mathematicians have decided that another
    type of distribution will better model the probability situation.
    I've introduced this in <xref ref="subsection-t-distribution"/>:
    the Student's <m>t</m>-distribution. This is very similar to the
    normal distribution: it is unimodl and decays to both sides with
    symmetric tails. The only main difference is the spread: Student's
    distribution is a bit wider that the normal distribution. This
    allows it to handle potential outliers more robustly. It's close
    enough to the normal distribution to use with the central limit
    theorem, but it hedges a bit and is less likely to fail with
    difficult data. <xref ref="figure-distribution-comparison"/> shows
    the comparison: the red curve is the normal distribution and the
    three other curves are Student's distributions. You can see the
    greater breadth, the larger tails, in Student's distribution.
  </p>
  <figure xml:id="figure-distribution-comparison">
    <caption>Caption</caption>
    <image width="80%">
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}[
          declare function={gamma(\z)= 2.506628274631*sqrt(1/\z)+ 0.20888568*(1/\z)^(1.5)+ 0.00870357*(1/\z)^(2.5)- (174.2106599*(1/\z)^(3.5))/25920- (715.6423511*(1/\z)^(4.5))/1244160)*exp((-ln(1/\z)-1)*\z;},
          declare function={student(\x,\n)= gamma((\n+1)/2.)/(sqrt(\n*pi) *gamma(\n/2.)) *((1+(\x*\x)/\n)^(-(\n+1)/2.));}
        ]
        \begin{axis}[
          axis lines=left,
          enlargelimits=upper,
          samples=50
          ]
          \addplot [thick, smooth, blue, domain=-6:6] {student(x,1)}; 
          \addplot [thick, smooth, green, domain=-6:6] {student(x,2)}; 
          \addplot [thick, smooth, orange, domain=-6:6] {student(x,5)}; 
          \addplot [thick, smooth, red, domain=-6:6] {student(x,100)}; 
        \end{axis}
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>
  <p>
    For proportions, we needed to build the sampling distribution with
    a mean and a standard deviation. Now we are going to use Student's
    distribution. I has a mean, but its second paramnter is called
    <sq>degrees of freedom</sq>, much like the
    <m>X^2</m>-distribution. The degrees of freedom will determine the
    shape of the curve around the mean. In the figure, the blue curve
    has <m>1</m> degree of freedom, the green curve has <m>2</m>
    degrees of freedom, and the orange curve has <m>5</m> degrees of
    freedom. As mentioned in the stretch outcome section, as the
    degrees of freedom parameter grows, Student's distribution become
    almost identical to a normal distribution with standar deviation
    <m>1</m>
  </p>
  <p>
    How is this parameter determined? For a single mean, it's a very
    easy bit of arithmetic. The degrees of freedom is simply one less
    than the number of cases. Our data has twenty cases, so the degree
    of freedom parameter is 19. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Once I've built a t-distribution, the setup is still the same. I
    want to talk about means and standard error, <m>z</m>-scores and
    <m>p</m>-values. I want to figure out if a sample statistic is
    within <m>95\%</m> of the data or not. I want to determine the
    middle <m>95\%</m> of this for a confidence intervals.
  </p> 
  <p>
    So what is the mathematics to do this? As before, the probability
    theorists have studied Student's -distribution and produced
    calculation techniques for us. Let me guide you through them.
    First, for hypothesis testing an <m>p</m>-values. 
    <ul>
      <li>
        I have a sample statistic for the mean: 23.385. 
      </li>
      <li>
        I have the null hypothesis, that the population mean is 21
        metres or less. 
      </li>
      <li>
        I have the alternative hypothesis that the population mean is
        more than 21 meters. 
      </li>
      <li>
        I want the standard error. For a single mean, this is a pretty
        straight forward calculation. The standard error is the
        standard deviation of the sample divided by the square root of
        the sample size. 
        <md>
          SE = \frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{n}}
        </md>
        Our sample has <m>20</m> cases and a standard deviation of
        <m>2.116</m>
        <md>
          SE = \frac{2.116}{\sqrt{20}} = 0.473
        </md>
      </li>
      <li>
        I can calculate the equivalent of a  <m>z</m>-score again if I
        wish. Since this is a <m>t</m>-distribution, the equivalent
        calculation here is called a <m>t</m>-score. Here is the
        calculation. 
        <md>
          z = \frac{\bar{x} - \mu}{SE} = \frac{23.385 -21}{0.473 =
          5.042
        </md>
        My sample is a bit more than <m>5</m> standard deviations
        above the mean. 
      </li>
      <li>
        Then I calculate the <m>p</m>-value. Historical, again, this
        was done from the <m>z</m>-score with tables for Student's
        <m>t</m>-distribution. Now we have R to calculate
        <m>p</m>-values direct as areas under the sampling
        distribution. R tells me that the <m>p</m>-value is <m>p =
        0.000367</m>. 
      </li>
      <li>
        This <m>p</m>-value is lower than basically any reasonable
        threshold of significance, I conclude that this statistic is
        significant. It provides evidence that, in the population, the
        mean length of a female adult blue whale is greater than 21
        metres. 
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
  <p>
    When we did hypothesis testing with normal distributions, we had
    to worry about one and two sided tests. The same is true here. In
    the example about blue whales, I only did a one-sided test.
    However, two-sided tests are also possible. The process is the
    same as before: if I want to make a one-sided test into a
    two-sided test, I just double the p-value. Visually, I'm, looking
    at both sides of the distibution, so the likeliness of being
    somewhere in one of the two tails doubles. The unlikely range is
    now only 2.5% of each side, not <m>5\%</m> of one tail. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Finally, let me talk about confidence intervals. I have the same
    starting information: the sample statistics, the degrees of
    freedom and the standard error. The setup for confidence
    intervals, as before, is an interval that starts below the sample
    mean and end above it. The distance out is some number times the
    standard error. For <m>95\%</m> for a normal distribution, this
    critical value was
    <m>1.96</m>, but that was for a normal distribution. The critical
    values for the <m>t</m>-distribution are different. R tells me
    that the appropriate critical value here is <m>2.09</m>. (It
    should make sense that the critical values are larger for
    Student's distribution since the tails are larger; I have to go
    out farther to capture the same amount of area. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Therefore, the confidence interval is the mean minus <m>2.09</m>
    times the standard error to the mean plus <m>2.09</m> times the standard
    error. In this case, it works out to <m>22.396</m> to
    <m>24.374</m>.
  </p>
  <p>
    What does this mean? It means based on this sample (and, as
    always, assuming the study doesn't have any bias or confounding
    variables) I am <m>95\%</m> confidend that the mean length of
    adult female blue whales across the entire population is between
    <m>22.396</m> and <m>24.374</m>. 
  </p>
</section>
